## 11. Service communication and routing

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  UI[Frontend / same-origin Next proxy] <-- REST and WebSocket --> GW[Python API gateway]
  GW <-- typed domain requests/results --> GO[Owning Go domain service]
  GO <-- private gRPC --> SIM[Python SUMO simulation]
  GO <-- private gRPC --> AI[Python intelligence]
  GW <-- typed writes and commit acknowledgments --> WR[Go DB writer]
  WR --> DB[(PostgreSQL)]
  GO --> Q[Read-only query access]
  Q --> DB
```

- Preserve `/api/v1` and `/ws/v1/live` public paths unless a versioned migration explicitly changes them. Enumerate every method/path, including aliases, owner, authorization, downstream RPC, response schema and persistence class.
- Go validates commands and service outputs before any durable result or operator-visible actionable state. Writer validates typed write envelopes again; never accept arbitrary SQL/table names from clients.
- Commit required durable command results before reporting durable success. Virtual signal dispatch uses durable intent and reconciliation rather than pretending an RPC and SQL transaction are atomic.
- Route run-specific commands and sockets to a single authoritative Go run owner. Add durable ownership/fencing and recovery before horizontal scaling; round-robin alone is not sufficient.
- Service deadlines, bounded retry, stream backpressure, tracing, idempotency and load/failure acceptance are mandatory (§32).
- [Audit route inventory](docs/UX-PRODUCTION-AUDIT.md) records every current handler and caller. Current implementation has no Python gateway or separate writer; this diagram is a target.

