## 32. Gateway, durable result and load-balancing requirements

### 32.1 Route and write ownership

The current frontend-to-Go flow is evidence, not the requested target. Implement the following explicit route owners; they may be Go modules within the existing traffic service initially:

| Route family | Go domain owner | Downstream dependency | Persistence policy |
|---|---|---|---|
| network, junctions, state, analysis, active recommendation | Configuration/state/intelligence query | Config, current run owner, private Python analysis | Read/transient; no write merely because an HTTP result exists |
| runs GET, audit GET, command outcome GET (new contract required) | Query owner | PostgreSQL read-only role | No mutation |
| runs POST, scenario start/reset | Lifecycle owner | Private simulation Reset; writer intent/run/status/audit | Durable business command |
| recommendation simulate/approve/modify/reject | Decision/safety owner | Private Compare/ApplyPlan; writer | Durable command result/audit; actuation intent before dispatch |
| mode and locks writes | Control owner | Writer plus current run owner | Durable mode/lock/result/audit before acknowledgment |
| replay start | Replay owner | Verified replay files; writer run/lifecycle | Durable lifecycle; frames transient |
| health | Gateway plus Go health aggregation | Domain owner, writer, compute and database health | Read; sampled health transitions only under an explicit policy |
| WebSocket live | Owning Go run/stream service via gateway | Simulation state, analysis, audit events | Frames transient; lifecycle/audit persistence is separate |

"Write Go results" means validated durable business-command outputs and audit/status records, not blindly storing arbitrary response bodies or every telemetry frame. This preserves the existing requirement to avoid frame-by-frame PostgreSQL writes. Define a persistence class for every new result; no double-write path is permitted.

### 32.2 Durable command protocol

1. Gateway authenticates and validates the public envelope; assign/propagate request ID, command ID, principal, deadline and target run/config/expected state version.
2. Go owner validates domain/safety/permissions and produces a typed result or intent. It returns a versioned write description through the gateway; private compute returns through its Go owner, not directly to the database.
3. Writer validates service identity, allowed command type, actor, schema, run ownership and payload hash. Use a database uniqueness constraint on command identity and atomically persist required domain changes, result/status and append-only audit. Same ID/same payload returns stored outcome; same ID/different payload returns conflict.
4. Pure durable commands are acknowledged to the frontend only after commit. A timeout after commit is an unknown transport outcome, not proof of rollback; use a permissioned outcome lookup.
5. For simulator effects, first persist validated intent, then dispatch using a durable payload-bound idempotency identity. Recheck fresh safety/run ownership immediately before dispatch. Persist applied/failed/unknown outcome after observation/acknowledgment. Accepted/pending-safe-boundary and applied are distinct states. Reconcile lost responses, restart and final-audit failure; do not roll back or repeat actuation blindly.
6. Expose useful operator recovery with command/run IDs and audit links, preserving drafts. Error responses retain structured code, retryability, dependency and whether an effect is known, absent or uncertain; internal stack details must not leak.

Only writer credentials can insert/update/delete business data; migrations/config initialization also have an explicit writer/deployment identity. Other services may have documented read-only credentials. Migration must inventory current inline SQL/sqlc call sites and cut over with one write authority at a time, tested rollback compatibility and backfill validation. Never introduce uncontrolled dual writes.

### 32.3 Load balancing and failure behavior

- Separate stateless gateway/query balancing from stateful run ownership. Current singleton Go state and Python Engine cannot safely be duplicated without ownership design.
- Use a durable run-owner lease/epoch, fencing of stale owners and deterministic routing for run commands/streams. Persist/reconstruct modes, locks and command outcomes. Define safe stop/recovery if the owner disappears; never allow two live controllers for one synthetic run.
- Gateway dispatch uses healthy upstreams, bounded connection/concurrency limits and readiness; unhealthy/overloaded services return explicit unavailable/rate-limit outcomes. The writer transaction and simulator CPU are measurable bottlenecks, not solved just by adding a proxy.
- Propagate one end-to-end deadline budget through gateway, Go, compute and writer. Cancel obsolete reads. Retry reads only within bounds with jitter; mutations require command idempotency/reconciliation. Define stream heartbeats, bounded fanout queues, drain/reconnect, event ordering and durable audit-cursor recovery.
- Preserve route/version compatibility and carry trace/request/command/run IDs through every hop. Log outcomes, not unnecessary full payloads.
- Benchmark at named client counts and replica topology, including at least two gateways, owner loss, writer loss, timeout-after-commit, duplicate commands and reconnect storms. Record p50/p95, throughput, errors, reconnect/recovery time, CPU/memory and DB pool/commit behavior. Targets in §25 remain targets until measured through the full revised path.
- No Kafka/Kubernetes/Redis/service mesh is mandated. Existing no-unrelated-microservices scope remains, with the user-required gateway/writer boundaries explicitly in scope.

## 33. Production UX acceptance contract

Apply this contract to Command Center, Network/comparison, junction drawer, Incidents, Emergency, Vision, Audit & Health, top-bar modes/locks and briefing. UI implementation is currently partial; see the audit for evidence.

| State or flow | Required acceptance |
|---|---|
| Loading / valid empty | Show bounded progress and meaningful empty actions; do not display zero/normal metrics as substitutes for missing data. |
| API / invalid payload / unexpected render error | Preserve status/code/request ID; validate response shapes; use a recoverable error boundary, visible error and safe next action. |
| Offline / slow / timeout | Distinguish internet-offline from local gateway/compute/DB failure, show stale timestamp/source, cancel obsolete requests, preserve drafts and provide bounded retry/reconciliation. |
| Service unavailable / maintenance / overload | Explicit degraded/maintenance/rate-limit state, affected capability, retry guidance and reachable replay when its dependencies are available. Missing health is unknown. |
| Authentication / permission / expiry | Shared production authenticates roles at gateway and Go; deny unauthorized commands server-side. 401 reauthentication and 403 denied flows preserve drafts and revalidate before submission. Demo persona selection is not auth. |
| Forms and submissions | Validate integer bounds/reasons using config; bind draft to run/recommendation version; disable conflicting pending/unsafe/stale/replay actions; retain input after failure and announce outcomes. |
| Destructive/replacing actions | Confirm scenario replacement/reset and draft loss; provide a known reset/recovery outcome. Do not promise undo for effects that cannot safely be undone. |
| Duplicate and uncertain commands | Stable payload-bound command identity across retry/reload; reconcile persisted outcome after a lost response. Distinguish pending, applied, rejected and unknown. |
| Navigation | Define URL/deep-link/back/refresh behavior, restore server mode/locks, manage focus and guard unsaved drafts. |
| Data truth and provenance | Every metric/explanation has actual run/model/config/horizon provenance; no static gains, fallback comparisons, healthy defaults or unconditional safety claims. Distinguish pending validation from applied safe result. |
| Audit history | Paginate all events, refresh external changes, link command/recommendation/run and show before/after/reason/actor/result. Never label ascending first 50 as latest 50. |
| Accessibility / responsive | Test narrow widths, long content, 200% text, 400% zoom and virtual keyboard; labels, keyboard tabs/dialog Escape/focus return, screen-reader announcements, measured contrast/target size, non-color statuses and reduced motion. Record measurement rather than assume compliance. |
| Theme | Dark-only remains allowed per existing product design; all controls/dialogs/error states must remain readable and consistent. Light/system theme is not automatically required. |
| Payments / device permissions | Not applicable: no such product flows were found. If later added, define success/failure/interruption or requested/denied/permanently-denied recovery before release. Do not invent implementations now. |

Run-scoped data must be invalidated when run or recommendation changes. A stale analysis cannot remain actionable merely because cached data exists. A comparison must match the active run/recommendation and immutable initial state; aggregate metrics must not masquerade as paired vehicle trajectories. Incident capacity, emergency stage/ETA/recovery and timing bounds come from real config/service outputs. Optional CV must explicitly say unavailable until implemented.

